WEBVTT

00:00:00.543 --> 00:00:24.887
<v Casey>Hey there, good to have you. It's Saturday, so Cariboo Local Affairs is the day. Today we've got Williams Lake's 36 Lakeview Avenue permit, two new units with no stated service-load reasoning, and the Pear Lake hazard report, flagged as preliminary and still missing a funding line for the next assessment. Plus Quesnel's water and sewer increases, about two dollars a month. Does a council's yes ever show its work? Stay with us.

00:00:38.362 --> 00:00:58.802
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Saturday, November 28th, 2026. Today's theme is Cariboo Local Affairs, and we're coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial.

00:00:59.802 --> 00:01:35.904
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're looking at how councils and districts make the calls that show up on our streets, in our schools, and on our tax bills, and what a town can actually see of those calls. Quick weather check. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's foggy and around minus one, with a high of 4 and a low of minus 2 today, and a 6 percent chance of precipitation. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around minus 4 and overcast, Williams Lake is around minus 2 and overcast, Quesnel is around minus 1 and overcast, and Redstone is around minus 4 and overcast.

00:01:36.269 --> 00:01:38.869
<v Casey>Headlines next, starting right here in the Cariboo.

00:01:40.269 --> 00:01:50.589
<v Riley>Community groups pitching in, a question over who gets a share of resource money, and provincial decisions landing in local classrooms and clinics. That's the shape of it today.

00:01:52.064 --> 00:02:16.688
<v Casey>Operation Red Nose is back in Williams Lake for the holiday season. Rides are by donation, running from 9 p.m. to 3 a.m. on set nights through December 31st, and the proceeds go to local youth sports. The drivers come from the groups the money supports: Williams Lake Minor Hockey's U18 female and integrated teams, the Cariboo Chilcotin Youth Fiddle Society, Cariboo Gymnastics Association, and Cariboo Cheer All-Stars.

00:02:17.138 --> 00:02:20.186
<v Riley>So the groups that get the money also supply the drivers.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:44.772
<v Riley>Thompson Rivers University carpentry students in Williams Lake spent a day on insulating concrete form, a wall system of foam panels with steel-reinforced concrete poured in. Home Hardware in Williams Lake donated the materials, and Fox Blocks led the seminar. The instructor, Mark Desautels, says the students wouldn't otherwise see the system until they were on a job site.

00:02:46.239 --> 00:03:02.223
<v Casey>On SOGI 123, which we flagged on Saturday and followed on Thursday, the B.C. Teachers' Federation is now asking which resources are meant, and whether books reflecting the diversity of families are on the list. Premier Lorne Doerkson says the program has become a lightning rod for division.

00:03:02.672 --> 00:03:04.568
<v Riley>So districts end up drawing the list.

00:03:06.201 --> 00:03:20.313
<v Riley>In Quesnel, the school board has a new chair. Trustee Tony Goulet beat fellow trustee Julie-Anne Runge at the board's inaugural meeting on November 18th. He replaces Lisa Boudreau, who chose not to run for chair this time around.

00:03:21.729 --> 00:03:48.585
<v Casey>Quesnel River Pulp is shut. Millar Western indefinitely closed the mill on November 9th, after announcing a curtailment in the summer. Cariboo-Prince George MP Todd Doherty calls that shameful, because the curtailment came weeks after the company received a federal loan of about $100 million. Doherty says the loan came with a 24-month employment plan the company has failed to meet, and he wants to know whether the federal minister knew the mill would close.

00:03:49.035 --> 00:03:53.203
<v Riley>Those are Doherty's claims. The loan terms are the part that can be checked.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:04:30.058
<v Riley>Ottawa released its first forest sector strategy on Friday. It's a $6.5 billion plan, and the money was already in the 2026 federal budget. Of that, $2 billion over six years goes to help businesses modernize and expand, $4 billion goes toward large-scale facilities and advanced products such as biofuels, and a $500 million Fibre Security Fund is aimed at fibre access and forest planning. Energy and Natural Resources Minister Tim Hodgson said B.C. softwood production fell by about half between 2017 and 2024.

00:04:30.508 --> 00:04:35.036
<v Casey>Half of B.C.'s softwood output gone in seven years, and the response is a budget line.

00:04:36.564 --> 00:05:16.178
<v Riley>Ten Central Interior local governments have signed a memorandum creating the Central Interior Resource Benefits Alliance. It will negotiate with the B.C. government for a share of revenue from major resource projects. Cariboo Regional District board chair Margo Wagner says the region is heading toward five of the largest mines in B.C., including Artemis Gold, Osisko Development's Barkerville project, Mount Polley, Gibraltar, and Spanish Mountain Gold. Her point is that the region gets the jobs and very little else. The alliance says it represents about 160,000 residents across roughly 131,000 square kilometres.

00:05:17.605 --> 00:05:40.885
<v Casey>Twelve northern B.C. First Nations closed a deal with TC Energy to take part of the company's Coastal GasLink stake. The purchase is backed by a $168 million loan guarantee from the Canada Indigenous Loan Guarantee Corporation, and the partnership is called the CGL First Nations Limited Partnership. In October, a separate partnership of five First Nations invested $65.3 million in the CGL subsidiary.

00:05:41.335 --> 00:05:45.399
<v Riley>That's ownership, and the guarantee is what got the money to the table.

00:05:47.186 --> 00:06:04.698
<v Riley>Quesnel's City Furniture is in its 34th year under the Wander family, and the franchise is marking 50 years in 2026, as we heard on Thursday. Her father brought twelve years of Prince George experience with the company when the family took over the Quesnel location.

00:06:06.207 --> 00:06:22.543
<v Casey>Fifteen-year-old Brooke Brown is co-directing Little Shop of Horrors at Williams Lake Studio Theatre with Jeff Richards. Richards says she is probably the youngest person to direct a mainstage production in the society's 72-year history. Brown also helped design the set and co-choreographed the show.

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:41.382
<v Riley>On November 11th, Quesnel residents gathered at the cenotaph by the legion for the annual Remembrance Day ceremony. The Royal Canadian Legion branch hosted a parade and muster that began around 10:45 a.m., and people stood in silence as poppies and wreaths were laid.

00:06:42.972 --> 00:07:10.324
<v Casey>Emily Lowan was sworn in as an MLA on Monday, for the first time, representing Saanich North and the Islands. She pledged to hold the new Conservative government to account. Jeremy Valeriote, re-elected for West Vancouver-Sea to Sky, was sworn in alongside her. Former Green MLA Adam Olsen, a member of the Tsartlip First Nation, draped the new members in striped Hudson's Bay blankets before the oath. The Greens kept their two seats, which holds them at the minimum for official party status.

00:07:10.773 --> 00:07:15.501
<v Riley>Two seats is the floor for official status. The pledge gets tested in the house.

00:07:16.989 --> 00:07:38.637
<v Casey>The B.C. Supreme Court dismissed a Charter challenge to a health ministry policy that lets religious hospitals opt out of medical assistance in dying. Chief Justice Ronald Skolrood found that when St. Paul's Hospital transfers a patient elsewhere for MAiD, the transfer doesn't violate the rights the challengers claimed. St. Paul's is a Catholic hospital run by Providence Health Care.

00:07:40.168 --> 00:08:05.736
<v Riley>Northern Health has issued a regional drug advisory that runs until December 4th. Overdoses needing medical help have increased across the region, and drug checking keeps finding medetomidine and benzodiazepines in the supply. Northern Health says those can make people very sleepy and hard to wake, and that some people need more than one dose of naloxone. Some symptoms may not improve with naloxone at all.

00:08:06.186 --> 00:08:10.154
<v Casey>The advice is the useful part: call for help, and don't count on one dose.

00:08:11.886 --> 00:08:37.366
<v Casey>The Prince George Humane Society has secured $2 million in commitments toward a $10 million Community Welfare Initiative for Northern B.C. The plan includes a Community Animal Hospital offering affordable veterinary care, preventive care, and spay and neuter programs, plus a mobile unit that will travel to smaller and Indigenous communities across the north. The work is being built in phases, starting with the hospital and the mobile unit.

00:08:38.484 --> 00:08:49.668
<v Riley>A lot of those decisions, from school boards to resource revenue to fee rises, are made in council and board rooms where residents usually only see the result. We'll pick that up now.

00:08:51.068 --> 00:09:15.676
<v Riley>Williams Lake Women's Centre offers crisis support, advocacy, and community programming for women and families in the Williams Lake area. When a situation turns urgent, the centre is there, and its programs keep people connected between the hard days. You can find out what they offer and how to give at wlwomenscentre.org. Giving Tuesday falls on December 1st this year, and that's a good day to think about it.

00:09:16.196 --> 00:09:23.372
<v Casey>A women's centre that provides crisis support and runs community programs is the kind of service a town only notices when it's gone.

00:09:23.472 --> 00:09:28.880
<v Casey>Our deep dive opens on a building permit in Williams Lake, which sounds small until you read the record.

00:09:30.280 --> 00:10:05.166
<v Casey>Start with 36 Lakeview Avenue in Williams Lake. On November 17th, council approved a development permit for an existing building. It will get two two-bedroom units upstairs, a renovated main floor for True Balance Float Space, facade updates, a deck over the laneway parking, five bike spaces, and a screened garbage area. Mayor Surinderpal Rathor said, "We need more development like this coming into the community." When a council says yes on that kind of reasoning, is it weighing the town's wider pressures, or deciding one parcel at a time? And what should the record show so a resident can tell which?

00:10:05.452 --> 00:10:30.668
<v Riley>A development permit is the council's written yes, with its conditions attached, and this one lists them. That's more specific than most of what councils put out. An existing building uses the water, sewer, and road that are already in the ground. Two units in a space described as underutilized is the lighter way to add homes than a new subdivision. Quick decisions like that keep a small council's agenda moving.

00:10:31.206 --> 00:10:39.074
<v Casey>Two units. The reasons on the record are a general preference and a claim of underuse. Neither says what the town is counting its needs against.

00:10:39.074 --> 00:10:45.834
<v Riley>Nobody's asking the council to write a regional plan into a permit. A permit answers the building in front of it.

00:10:46.398 --> 00:11:03.054
<v Casey>Then the approval should name what it was read against. Staff retention, wildfire hazard, household bills. Those are the pressures a council is actually sitting on. The approval, as reported, names facade work, a deck, and bike racks. It says nothing about the water and sewer draw of two new households.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:14.200
<v Riley>Two households is a small draw on any system. Whether it's small on that particular system is a question staff can answer in a sentence, and the sentence belongs in the file.

00:11:14.814 --> 00:11:32.646
<v Casey>Then the file has to exist, and a resident has to know to ask for it. A resident who wants to check the yes has to find the staff report, match it to the zoning, and read it against a hazard map the permit doesn't mention. Most people won't. A council that wants residents to trust a yes puts its reasoning next to the yes.

00:11:33.218 --> 00:11:46.234
<v Riley>Every extra line is staff time on every permit, and a small town's planning office doesn't have many people in it. Keep the detailed lines for the permits that change the load. A bike rack doesn't need a hydraulic note.

00:11:46.812 --> 00:11:55.516
<v Casey>Then the threshold is the whole fight. Who decides a permit changes the load? If it's the council on the night, the threshold is set by whoever is already in the room.

00:11:56.048 --> 00:12:02.848
<v Riley>Which is why the threshold should be written down before the night. Set a rule, publish it, and let the rule do the sorting.

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:09.172
<v Casey>A rule can be published and still never be applied. That's the test I'd want to see before I trusted it.

00:12:09.272 --> 00:12:43.034
<v Casey>Residents are already measuring some of these pressures without waiting for a permit. On November 21st, twenty-eight healthcare professionals gathered at the Osprey Nest for the Williams Lake Social Collective's first healthcare mixer. Optometrists, dentists, respiratory therapists, nurses, occupational therapists, pedorthists. The organizers, Ashleigh Cire and Jerusha Alvares, both work in health care. They named emergency department closures during staffing shortages as the reason recruitment and retention were on their minds. The group raised more than $800 for future events.

00:12:43.034 --> 00:12:47.154
<v Riley>Eight hundred dollars, and a room full of people who work the same shifts.

00:12:47.254 --> 00:13:01.374
<v Riley>Those people already know each other from the emergency room floor. This gives them a reason to stay in town on a night off. A community holding its own ground without a motion is a strength, and it shouldn't wait for a council to start.

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:07.742
<v Casey>It was one evening. That's evidence of concern. It isn't a plan that council decisions have to answer to.

00:13:08.311 --> 00:13:40.893
<v Riley>It isn't a plan, and the city isn't leaving staffing to a mixer. The Cariboo Living Healthcare Landing Program is a community-based recruitment and retention effort for Williams Lake and 100 Mile House. It's funded through the Cariboo Chilcotin Regional Hospital District, run in partnership with the City of Williams Lake and the District of 100 Mile House, and it has a housing support component. The Tribune reports it has been giving council updates on physician recruitment and retention for three years.

00:13:41.470 --> 00:14:00.750
<v Casey>Three years of updates. Updates on what, exactly? CBC has covered the city hiring two healthcare landing co-ordinators to attract long-term and locum doctors, in competition with similar-sized rural communities. Those are jobs with a target. The number that matters is how many of the people they recruit are still in town after two or three years.

00:14:01.329 --> 00:14:23.097
<v Riley>The program doesn't report an overall retention percentage. It does report that of 77 people supported in one 10-month period, 31, about 40 per cent, kept coming back to the community. That's repeat visits, a leading signal. Retention lags by years, while a mixer's effect shows up in the next month. Those are different instruments, and you need both.

00:14:23.703 --> 00:14:33.591
<v Casey>Then publish the lagging number when it exists, and don't let the mixer stand in for it in the meantime. An evening that raises $800 is a fine thing. It isn't a retention rate.

00:14:34.141 --> 00:14:45.373
<v Riley>The program should answer that test. My point stands, though. A doctor who arrives with a housing contact and colleagues they've already met is a better bet than one who arrives to a blank page.

00:14:45.887 --> 00:14:59.271
<v Casey>It's a bet. The city is making it, and the residents running the mixer are making it. Ask the program how many recruits are still in Williams Lake after three years. If the answer is a count, that's a result. If it's a list of events, it's a calendar.

00:14:59.371 --> 00:15:35.833
<v Casey>We covered who should own post-fire slope risk on October 30th, so the new piece here is timing. The preliminary Pear Lake report is dated October 8th and was released November 9th. The Tribune says it rests largely on aerial reconnaissance from August. It finds likely moderate to high impacts on homes, highways, the CN rail line, and a BC Hydro substation, from flooding, debris flows, and landslides. The fire burned 165,909 hectares near Clinton, destroyed more than 120 structures, and affected 28 watersheds. We had the Clinton numbers on Tuesday, and the hazard report is what comes after them.

00:15:36.346 --> 00:15:55.834
<v Riley>The mechanism is plain. Burned ground sheds water, so the first heavy rain carries soil and debris downhill onto whatever sits below. The report was done on an expedited basis so a Level 4 Detailed Assessment can start. A council that gets a ranked list can decide which culvert to look at before the next storm instead of after it.

00:15:56.404 --> 00:16:08.552
<v Casey>The TNRD's Pear Lake reconnaissance records sediment-laden floods and debris flows that have already happened, along Pavilion-Clinton Road and around Jesmond. So the hazard isn't a forecast. It has already arrived in the field.

00:16:08.552 --> 00:16:13.728
<v Riley>Which is why the ranking matters. It tells you where it has already gone wrong and where it can go next.

00:16:14.343 --> 00:16:31.127
<v Casey>A ranking isn't mitigation. The word "preliminary" matters. A reconnaissance flown in August shows where the damage is visible from the air. It doesn't show which culvert is plugged, or what a landowner's slope can hold. The report was done fast so the detailed work can start. Who pays for the detailed work?

00:16:31.456 --> 00:16:40.608
<v Riley>The report names the Level 4 as the next step, so the next step is already defined. A defined schedule is easier to hold people to than a promise.

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:59.142
<v Casey>Defined isn't funded. The reports I can find mention no funding for the Level 4 assessment. The report puts a rail line and a substation in the elevated column with no detailed assessment behind either one yet. A month went by between the report's date and its release. The next storm didn't wait for the paperwork.

00:16:59.693 --> 00:17:09.565
<v Riley>Then a substation is the first thing a detailed assessment should price. A rail line is the second. That's a sequencing choice, and a council can ask for it in writing.

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:14.958
<v Casey>And private land? Whose slope is it, and who answers for it? The sources don't name anyone.

00:17:15.458 --> 00:17:50.034
<v Riley>Bills are the other end of the same pipe. Quesnel's Finance Department put the 2027 fee proposal to council on November 24th. My Cariboo Now reports water up 4 per cent, $13.08 for a single-family dwelling. The Quesnel Cariboo Observer reports sewer up 3.5 per cent, $11.64 for the same household. Together that's $24.72 a year, about two dollars a month. Last year sewer rose 5 per cent, $15.36. Those fees pay for pipes and plants, and when the cost of keeping them rises, the fee moves with it.

00:17:50.716 --> 00:17:59.020
<v Casey>Predictable is the part that flatters the council. A household on a fixed income can plan for four percent. The increases recur, and so does the bill.

00:17:59.702 --> 00:18:06.790
<v Riley>A surprise is what a deferred pipe hands you. Small, predictable steps beat the lump sum that arrives after the failure.

00:18:07.321 --> 00:18:14.165
<v Casey>Maybe. But the step is still paid by the people in the house, and nobody asked them to choose between a steady rise and a sudden one.

00:18:14.165 --> 00:18:20.549
<v Riley>Lakeview is infill, though. Reusing an existing building is the least likely way to add a new pipe to a town.

00:18:21.071 --> 00:18:34.879
<v Casey>Least likely isn't zero. The approval doesn't say how much the two units draw. Quesnel's rise is the cost of service, and it reaches a household whatever the council meant by it. A bill is the one part of a decision a household sees for certain.

00:18:35.469 --> 00:18:40.717
<v Riley>Then the practical question is the note itself. Who writes it? Council, staff, or the applicant?

00:18:41.072 --> 00:18:43.752
<v Casey>Not the applicant. They're the ones who want the yes.

00:18:44.438 --> 00:18:57.662
<v Riley>Staff, then. Staff already write the report council reads, so the service-load line can live there, next to the hazard status and the staffing note. Council asks for the line, and the minutes record that it was asked for.

00:18:58.199 --> 00:19:04.943
<v Casey>And a note written after the vote is a note about the vote. The note has to come before the motion, or it's a receipt.

00:19:05.567 --> 00:19:18.079
<v Riley>Then the timing goes in the bylaw too. Staff submit the line seven days before the meeting, and it sits in the agenda package where a resident can read it. The town can write that rule without a new department.

00:19:18.607 --> 00:19:29.575
<v Casey>Now test whether it changes anything. A note that says the service draw is small and the hazard status is unchanged can be filled in every time without ever changing a vote. Decoration is one outcome.

00:19:30.132 --> 00:19:41.964
<v Riley>So run it for a few months with the lines written, and see whether an approval ever gets narrowed, conditioned, or sent back. If none ever does, the lines were decoration, and the council should say so.

00:19:42.573 --> 00:19:55.725
<v Casey>Another outcome is a council that writes the lines, approves the same two units, and nobody can say whether it weighed anything. A note shows the council was asked. It doesn't show the council weighed the town. That part is still unproven.

00:19:56.378 --> 00:20:00.554
<v Riley>Then the next approval is the first test, and it costs one line in a staff report.

00:20:01.076 --> 00:20:18.516
<v Casey>So the question stays open: does a short note on service draw and hazard status change what a council decides, or only what it writes down? If you have a story tip or a permit file you'd like read, send it to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. Mind the fog out at Horsefly, and enjoy the rest of your Saturday.

00:20:19.115 --> 00:20:38.243
